From 06ed5691f3e0e0f55d2440ff388f165de96e8846 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Campos Ramos <Capitain_Jack@yahoo.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 17:15:17 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] drm: Commit KWin's frame sequential twin for the next
 refresh

KWin's own frame sequential twins need a commit on every refresh, each for
the refresh right after the last one; when nothing has changed, the pair on
screen is re-committed with its other eye and nothing is rendered for it.

When the render loop predicted that compositing takes about a refresh or
longer, it queued frames two refreshes ahead (triple buffering, which its
hysteresis keeps until ten frames in a row are predicted below 95 % of a
refresh). On a still picture only the page flip schedules the next frame,
so the number of frames in flight stays what it was:
- With one frame in flight, each re-commit landed two refreshes after the
  last one with the eye due on the refresh in between, so a commit landed
  only on every second refresh and every one showed the same eye, until a
  repaint asked for by something else added a second frame.
- With two frames in flight the eyes alternated, but each frame landed a
  refresh after the one its eye was chosen for (a wrong-eye slot on every
  refresh by KWin's own count), and the order of the eyes changed whenever
  the number of frames in flight did.
A prediction near one refresh, as heavier rendering before the twin leaves
it while it decays, kept switching between the two. Under llvmpipe in a
virtual machine that showed one eye for up to 240 refreshes in a row and
swapped the eyes between still pictures, after DisplayPort's twin and after
KWin's other stereo twins alike.

In KWin's frame sequential layouts the render loop now has one frame
pending at most (DrmOutput::maxPendingFrames()), and RenderLoop leaves
triple buffering at once when it may have only one frame pending, so every
frame, a held pair's re-commit included, targets the refresh after the
last one. Other layouts, DisplayPort's frame sequential stereo among them,
keep two frames.

testMockDrmFrameSequential's testHeldPairTargetsNextRefresh predicts 40 ms
of rendering and checks how many refreshes ahead the render loop targets
after each page flip: two in the 2D mode before and after the twin, one on
the twin, after a held pair's first eye and after its second.

